#: templates/admin/docs.php:112
msgid "Defaults, CSV options, history cleanup and privacy options (administrators only)."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:114
msgid "This page."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:116
msgid "<strong>Requirements:</strong> WordPress 6.2 or newer and PHP 7.4 or newer. Excel export also needs the PHP <code>ZipArchive</code> extension, which almost every host provides."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:121
msgid "Open <strong>UserFerry → Export users</strong>. The wizard walks you through four steps; you can go back to any finished step from the step bar."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:124
#: templates/admin/export.php:14
#: templates/admin/header.php:28
#: templates/admin/history.php:49
#: templates/admin/templates.php:85
msgid "Users"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:125
msgid "Choose <strong>All users</strong>, <strong>By role</strong>, <strong>By registration date</strong> or <strong>Specific users</strong>. With specific users you search and tick people from a paged list; your ticks are kept while you search and change pages."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:126
msgid "Optionally narrow the result with a search term (it matches username, email, display name and profile slug) and choose how rows are sorted. The live counter under the options shows how many users will be exported."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:129
#: templates/admin/export.php:15
#: templates/admin/templates.php:93
msgid "Fields"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:130
msgid "Tick the fields you want. Each ticked field becomes a column, shown in the <strong>Column order</strong> strip at the bottom where you can reorder or remove columns. See “Choosing fields” below."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:133
#: templates/admin/docs.php:218
#: templates/admin/export.php:16
#: templates/admin/history.php:48
msgid "Format"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:134
msgid "Pick CSV, Excel (XLSX), JSON or XML and set the file name. The date and a short random code are appended automatically."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:137
#: templates/admin/export.php:17
msgid "Review & export"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:138
msgid "Check the summary, optionally save everything as a template, then click <strong>Run export</strong>. A progress bar shows each batch; when the file is ready the download starts by itself and a <strong>Download file</strong> button stays on screen."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:142
msgid "<strong>Large sites:</strong> exports run in batches (200 users per request by default), so even sites with hundreds of thousands of users finish without timeouts. Keep the tab open until the file is ready."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:148
msgid "Fields are listed in three groups. Each field shows a readable name and, underneath, its real database key so two similar names are never confused."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:151
msgid "User ID, username, email, first and last name, display name, nickname, role, website, registered date, biographical info, profile slug and status."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:152
#: templates/admin/partials/field-picker.php:61
msgid "Custom profile fields"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:153
msgid "Every other meta key found in the user meta table — data saved by ACF, Ultimate Member, WooCommerce (billing and shipping), BuddyPress, Meta Box, JetEngine, registration form plugins and your own code."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:155
msgid "Keys that begin with an underscore, admin screen preferences, dismissed notices and plugin housekeeping. This group is collapsed by default; click “Show” if you need something from it."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:157
msgid "Why each field appears only once"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:159
msgid "Plugins such as ACF save two meta keys for every field: the value (<code>district</code>) and a hidden reference (<code>_district</code>) that only stores the field’s internal ID. Listing both would show every field twice, so the scanner drops the reference key whenever the real value exists, and skips <code>first_name</code>, <code>last_name</code>, <code>nickname</code> and <code>description</code> because they are already in the WordPress profile group."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:160
msgid "The field list is cached for 12 hours. After installing a plugin that adds user fields — or after the first user fills in a new form field — click <strong>Scan custom fields</strong>."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:162
#: templates/admin/partials/field-picker.php:103
msgid "Column order"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:163
msgid "Columns are added in the order you tick them. Drag a column in the strip to move it, or focus it with the Tab key and use the left and right arrow keys. Press Delete or Backspace to remove it."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:164
msgid "How values are written"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:165
msgid "Simple lists (for example checkbox values) are joined with commas. Complex values are written as JSON so no data is lost. Roles use their readable names."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:170
msgid "A template stores who to include, the fields in their column order, the format and the file name. Example: a template named “Subscribers — login & education” with role <em>Subscriber</em> and the fields Username, Email, Last name and Education."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:171
#: templates/admin/templates.php:19
msgid "Create a template"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:172
msgid "Either click <strong>New template</strong> on the Templates screen and fill in the editor, or build an export in the wizard and use <strong>Save as new template</strong> on the review step."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:173
msgid "Use a template"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:174
msgid "<strong>Export now</strong> runs the template immediately and downloads the file. <strong>Open in wizard</strong> (or “Start from a template” at the top of the wizard) loads it with every field already ticked, so you can adjust anything before exporting."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:175
msgid "Edit, duplicate and delete"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:176
msgid "Use the pencil, copy and bin buttons on each template card. When you load a template into the wizard and change it, the review step also offers <strong>Update</strong> to overwrite the original. Deleting a template does not delete past exports."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:181
msgid "Open <strong>Import users</strong> to create or update accounts from a file. Any file exported by this plugin — CSV, Excel, JSON or XML — can be imported again unchanged, so you can move users between sites, or export, edit in Excel and import the changes back."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:184
#: templates/admin/import.php:16
msgid "Upload file"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:185
msgid "Drop a .csv, .xlsx, .json or .xml file onto the upload area. The first row (or the keys of each JSON/XML record) must contain the column names. Comma, semicolon and tab separated CSV files are detected automatically."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:188
#: templates/admin/import.php:17
msgid "Map columns"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:189
msgid "Every column is matched to a field automatically — by its name, its meta key or common words such as “Email” or “Surname”. Change any row, choose “Don’t import” to ignore a column, or pick “New custom field…” to save it under a meta key of your choice. At least one column must be Email, Username or User ID."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:192
#: templates/admin/import.php:18
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:193
msgid "Decide how existing users are found (email, username or ID), whether they are updated or skipped, whether new users are created, the role for new users without a Role column, whether empty cells are ignored, and whether new users receive the WordPress welcome email."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:197
msgid "Rows are processed in small batches with a progress bar. The summary shows how many users were created, updated, skipped or failed, with the row number and reason for every problem."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:200
msgid "Good to know"
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:202
msgid "Usernames and registration dates of existing users never change. The registration date is only used for new users."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:203
msgid "Roles may be written as names (Subscriber, Editor) or keys (subscriber). Several roles can be separated by commas. You can only assign roles you are allowed to assign, and your own role is never changed."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:204
msgid "Passwords: a “Password (plain text)” column sets the password. A “Password hash” column — available when “Allow exporting password hashes” is on — keeps users’ existing passwords when moving them between WordPress sites. Without either, new users get a strong random password."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:205
msgid "Values written as JSON by the export (complex custom fields) are restored as structured data. Simple lists exported as “a, b” are stored as text."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:206
msgid "Users are not emailed about profile or password changes made by an import."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:207
msgid "The uploaded file is never kept: its rows are stored in the protected plugin folder only while the import runs."
msgstr ""

#: templates/admin/docs.php:210
msgid "<strong>Tip:</strong> run an export of your users before a large import, so you can restore them if a column was mapped incorrectly."
msgstr ""

